{
  "teamId": 143,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Philadelphia Phillies were a team of contradictions — a club that could look brilliant one week and baffling the next, yet somehow found a way to scratch out 88 wins and punch their ticket to October. They opened the year sputtering, going a combined 12-19 through March and April, with costly double plays and squandered opportunities threatening to sink the season before it truly began. But Brandon Marsh, Edmundo Sosa, and a resurgent Bryson Stott provided early sparks that kept the faith alive in Citizens Bank Park.\n\nMay and June became the heartbeat of Philadelphia's season. Stott emerged as the team's most consistent clutch performer, launching walk-off shots and delivering pivotal extra-base hits week after week. Justin Crawford announced himself as a legitimate contributor, and Kyle Schwarber reached the 30-home-run plateau by late June. A wild June 23rd game — featuring a crushing blow off Orion Kerkering followed by a Stott three-run homer — became a microcosm of the whole season: never easy, never pretty, but ultimately survivable. Derek Hill's dramatic home run the very next day underscored that this roster had depth no one had fully anticipated.\n\nAugust was the Phillies' finest hour. Going 21-7, the team transformed from a fringe playoff contender into one of the hottest clubs in baseball. Edmundo Sosa's grand slam on August 28th — a 0.769 WPA thunderbolt — was the signature moment of the entire regular season, the kind of play that earns a team belief in itself. Hill added his own grand slam earlier in the month, and Schwarber kept mashing. September proved that August had borrowed from the future; the rotation cracked and the bullpen wobbled, with a brutal three-game September 11th collapse against Atlanta leaving the team limping toward the finish line at 10-14 for the month.\n\nThe Wild Card round brought the Phillies face-to-face with Atlanta, and it ended the way so many Philadelphia Octobers have — in heartbreak. Realmuto's triple gave the city a pulse on September 29th, but Austin Riley's three-run homer off a faltering bullpen extinguished the flame. Games two and three saw Harper and Bohm go deep, and Cristopher Sánchez pitched with grit, but the Braves were simply the better team in October. The 2026 Phillies were a squad of real moments and real talent who burned brightest in August and faded when the lights shone brightest of all.",
